 Green/Red percentage, or "toxic" diagnosis is produced.
 
 For recognised chats, the overall *excerpt label* follows this rule, in order:
@@ -51,6 +51,27 @@ original card flow exports one 1080×1350 PNG; edited names and numeric local
 highlights remain visible in its preview before sharing. This presentation
 does not change the four Jev questions or their scoring rules.
 
+Each person gets a **visual animal sticker for a counted pattern**, using only
+the part of a parsed chat submitted to Jev. Both need at least three text
+messages; otherwise both receive a neutral sticker. In this order: a **dog**
+starts strictly more of at least three observed conversations than the other
+person (ties get no starter sticker); a **hedgehog** has a median of at least
+15 words per message; a **mouse** has a median of at most 6; everyone else
+gets a **cat**. The numeric observation and `n` (or starts out of conversations)
+appear beside the sticker. Animals represent message patterns, never identities
+or personality. Raw formats get neutral stickers because their speakers cannot
+be verified. For truncated chats the sticker uses only the submitted excerpt;
+the separate existing local statistics can describe the full parsed chat.
+
+The shared duo story uses only the four existing ordinal scores, in order:
+tension >= 2 gives a friction story; otherwise clarity <= 1 gives a clarifying
+story; otherwise warmth and reciprocity >= 2 give a warm story; otherwise it
+describes an everyday excerpt. Raw or invalid scores, or fewer than six sent
+messages, give a neutral story. Relevant scores and the observed message count
+are shown; future-sounding lines are explicitly speculative. The mission uses
+the existing `conversationIdea` rules. A short CSS reveal animates the sticker
+duo without delaying results; reduced-motion settings remove the animation.
+
 The result shows an original two-chat-bubble illustration for each of the
 four possible labels, a two-axis map of Jev's **warmth** (horizontal, 0–3)
 and **visible tension** (vertical, 0–3), and a local bar chart of each
@@ -93,7 +114,8 @@ The notice above the button explains both cases before any request is made.
